    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 3yr CAGR 12.7% vs EPS 2.1% (and owner FCF -2.9%): book value is not a usable growth anchor for Ulta -- it is shaped by ~$0.9-1.5B/yr of buybacks against ~$1.0B owner FCF, so equity moves with the pace of repurchases, not with earnings power. All 5yr CAGRs start from fiscal 2020 (FYE Jan-2021), the COVID store-closure trough, and are inflated (EPS 52.5%, revenue 15.0%).",
    "notes": "Labels follow Metrics.csv (calendar year of FYE). Owner-FCF series = reported FCF less SBC (no interest-income strip; Ulta has held little net cash and earned trivial interest). SBC is ~0.3-0.5% of revenue, so owner and reported FCF CAGRs are almost identical -- SBC is not funding the growth. Owner FCF has gone sideways ($1.13B fiscal 2022 -> $1.03B fiscal 2025) as operating margin fell from ~15.5% to ~12.4% while revenue grew ~6.7%/yr; EPS growth (2.1%/yr) is almost entirely buyback-driven. Selected growth 6.7% (3yr revenue) is the near-term anchor, decelerated toward terminal in the base."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Ulta delivers the raised fiscal 2026 guide (sales ~+7%, operating income ~+9%), then settles into mid-single-digit growth: ~3% comps from loyalty (45M+ Ulta Beauty Rewards members), new-brand launches and wellness, 1-2% from ~50 net new US stores a year plus Space NK's UK/US rollout, fading to 3% by year 10 as the US store base saturates. 'Ulta Beauty Unleashed' cost work holds adj EBITDA ~15.5% -- recovering a little of the 2022-25 compression from shrink, wage and marketing investment, but not back to the post-COVID peak while Sephora-at-Kohl's and Amazon Premium Beauty keep promotions elevated. Capex trends to ~2.9-3.0% of sales as remodels dominate new builds.",
    "bull": "Prestige/mass beauty stays the most resilient consumer category and Ulta keeps taking share: Sephora's Kohl's partnership underdelivers as Kohl's shrinks, Target shop-in-shops and the Ulta marketplace/UB Media retail-media business scale, and Space NK becomes a credible second growth engine (UK plus US expansion, Middle East franchise). Growth runs 6-7% for five years and operating leverage, retail-media margin and lower shrink take adj EBITDA to ~17% -- between today and the fiscal 2022 high. Terminal growth 3%, 16x cap.",
    "bear": "Beauty normalises after a multi-year boom and Ulta's moat thins: Sephora (Kohl's ~850 shops plus own stores), Amazon Premium Beauty, TikTok Shop dupes and mass-channel prestige launches pressure traffic and force promotional loyalty spend. Fiscal 2026 lands at the low end of guidance (H1 already banked), then comps go flat-to-negative and growth is store-count only (~2%). Deleverage on a high fixed-cost lease base takes adj EBITDA from 15.1% to 13.0%; Space NK proves a low-margin distraction. Terminal growth 2%, 12x cap (a mature, share-donating retailer)."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Ulta is a mature, cash-generative specialty retailer with negligible SBC (0.3% of revenue), so the house (SBC-expensed) and street bases sit close together; the valuation turns on margin durability against Sephora/Amazon competition and on how long mid-single-digit growth lasts. Buybacks shrink the count ~4-5%/yr but are value-neutral in this model -- they return the same owner FCF the DCF already counts.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Margin: each 1pp of adj EBITDA margin is worth roughly $40/share; the base holds ~15.6% vs 14.8% GAAP EBITDA in fiscal 2025.",
      "Competition: Sephora at Kohl's, Amazon Premium Beauty and mass-channel prestige compress traffic and promotional economics.",
      "Seasonal balance sheet: net debt is taken at the Q2 trough (2026-08-01); at fiscal year-end Ulta is typically ~$400M net cash (~$10-13/share).",
      "Terminal value carries ~55-60% of EV; the 15x base cap binds (Gordon at 9%/2.5% is ~15.8x).",
      "Space NK integration and international expansion are unproven at scale."
    ]
  },
